### What is the Top K Numbers problem?

Given an unsorted array of numbers and a value K, find the K largest numbers. The result does not need to be sorted. This is the foundational Top K Elements problem that appears in many variations: top K frequent, top K closest, and K-th largest.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class TopKFinder {

    public static List<Integer> findTopK(int[] amounts, int k) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();

        for (int amount : amounts) {
            if (minHeap.size() < k) {
                minHeap.offer(amount);
            } else if (amount > minHeap.peek()) {
                minHeap.poll();
                minHeap.offer(amount);
            }
        }

        return new ArrayList<>(minHeap);
    }
}
```

### Why use a min-heap instead of a max-heap?

The counter-intuitive choice is that to find the K largest numbers, you use a min-heap, not a max-heap. The root of the min-heap is the smallest of the K numbers you are currently keeping. This means the root is the weakest candidate in your kept set, so deciding whether a new number deserves a place is a single O(1) peek: if the new number is larger than the root, it replaces the root. If you used a max-heap, the root would be the largest kept number, and you would have no efficient way to find and evict the weakest.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

public class WhyMinHeap {

    public static void explain() {
        // Min-heap of size 3, holding [5, 8, 10]
        // Root = 5 (smallest of the 3 largest)
        // New number 7: 7 > 5? Yes -> evict 5, insert 7
        // Heap becomes [7, 8, 10]
        // New number 3: 3 > 7? No -> discard
        // Heap stays [7, 8, 10]

        // If we used a max-heap of size 3, root = 10
        // New number 7: is it one of the top 3? We cannot tell from the root!
        // We would need to inspect all elements -> defeats the purpose
    }
}
```

### How does the keep-or-discard decision work?

For the first K elements, push them all into the heap to fill it to size K. For each subsequent element, compare it with the heap root (the smallest of the K largest). If the new element is larger, poll the root and offer the new element. If it is smaller or equal, discard it. After processing all elements, the heap contains exactly the K largest numbers.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class KeepOrDiscard {

    public static void trace() {
        int[] amounts = {100, 500, 200, 800, 300, 900, 150};
        int k = 3;
        PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();

        // Fill first K=3: heap = [100, 500, 200] -> root = 100
        // 800 > 100: evict 100, insert 800 -> heap = [200, 500, 800] -> root = 200
        // 300 > 200: evict 200, insert 300 -> heap = [300, 500, 800] -> root = 300
        // 900 > 300: evict 300, insert 900 -> heap = [500, 800, 900] -> root = 500
        // 150 < 500: discard
        // Final: [500, 800, 900] = top 3 largest

        for (int amount : amounts) {
            if (minHeap.size() < k) minHeap.offer(amount);
            else if (amount > minHeap.peek()) {
                minHeap.poll();
                minHeap.offer(amount);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Top 3: " + minHeap);
    }
}
```

```text
Top 3: [500, 800, 900]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(n log K). Each of the n elements costs at most one peek (O(1)) and one poll-plus-offer (O(log K)). When K is much smaller than n, this is significantly better than O(n log n) sorting. Space is O(K) for the heap.

| Approach | Time | Space | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Sort then take last K | O(n log n) | O(1) or O(n) | Simple but slower |
| Min-heap of size K | O(n log K) | O(K) | Optimal for small K |
| Quickselect | O(n) average | O(1) | Best average but O(n^2) worst |
| Max-heap of all elements | O(n + K log n) | O(n) | Wastes space |

### How does this compare to quickselect?

Quickselect finds the K-th largest in O(n) average time but O(n^2) worst case. The min-heap approach guarantees O(n log K) worst case. For streaming data where elements arrive one at a time, quickselect is not applicable because it requires the full array, while the heap processes each element as it arrives.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

public class QuickSelectComparison {

    // Quickselect: requires full array, O(n) average, O(n^2) worst
    // Min-heap: streaming, O(n log K) guaranteed

    // When to use which:
    // - Full array available, need K-th element: quickselect
    // - Streaming data, need top K: min-heap
    // - Need guaranteed worst case: min-heap
    // - K is close to n: sorting may be simpler
}
```

### What about the K smallest (symmetric problem)?

By symmetry, to find the K smallest numbers, use a max-heap of size K. The root is the largest of the K smallest numbers. A new number replaces the root only if it is smaller. The same O(n log K) time and O(K) space apply.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class BottomKFinder {

    public static List<Integer> findBottomK(int[] amounts, int k) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());

        for (int amount : amounts) {
            if (maxHeap.size() < k) {
                maxHeap.offer(amount);
            } else if (amount < maxHeap.peek()) {
                maxHeap.poll();
                maxHeap.offer(amount);
            }
        }
        return new ArrayList<>(maxHeap);
    }
}
```

### What are edge cases?

Edge cases include K equal to zero (return empty), K equal to n (return all elements), K greater than n (return all elements), and an array with duplicate values.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

import java.util.List;

public class EdgeCases {

    public static void test() {
        System.out.println(TopKFinder.findTopK(new int[]{5, 3, 8}, 0));  // []
        System.out.println(TopKFinder.findTopK(new int[]{5, 3, 8}, 3));  // [3, 5, 8]
        System.out.println(TopKFinder.findTopK(new int[]{5, 5, 5}, 2));  // [5, 5]
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should mention that for very large K close to n, sorting is simpler. For distributed systems, each machine can maintain its own top-K min-heap and the results can be merged by inserting all candidates into a final size-K min-heap. For real-time dashboards at scale, approximate algorithms like count-min sketch can provide top-K estimates with bounded error.

```java
// Senior considerations:
// 1. K close to n: just sort, simpler code
// 2. Distributed top-K: each node keeps size-K heap, merge at coordinator
// 3. Real-time approximate: count-min sketch + min-heap
// 4. Thread safety: wrap heap in synchronized block or use concurrent structure
// 5. Banking: use BigDecimal for amounts, Comparator.reverseOrder() for max-heap
```
